        "synthesis": {
          "model_calls": 1,
          "phase": "synthesis",
          "plan": {
            "A": "Develop the dense scalar kernel from eb932fb3353c4666: four-row fusion reduced measured time from 23.39 ms for the earlier one-row program to 13.51 ms. Investigate small fusion factors and obstacle-event handling, retaining conditional modular subtraction and linear DP storage. Prepare a fusion kernel suitable for C's tiles: accept top and left boundaries, preserve every row's final right boundary, and emit the bottom boundary. Handle incomplete row groups, partial tiles, duplicate obstacles, and blocked endpoints exactly. Deliver a complete admissible program and measured results, not an assumed speedup.",
            "B": "Develop the ARM64 modular prefix-scan kernel from b3363e491a4646a9. Its complete program measured 15.37 ms versus 23.04 ms for the preceding sparse/scalar hybrid, but the observations do not isolate which execution path contributed. Adapt NEON scans to obstacle-free tile-row segments with an explicit incoming left carry, zero carry after obstacles, and scalar tails. Reduce modulo MOD at every scan stage to keep lanes bounded; do not mark the original dependent recurrence as independent SIMD work. Separately retain exact factorial/binomial counting for empty grids and sparse first-obstacle subtraction where its preprocessing and quadratic work are justified. Bound reduction accumulation and use overflow-safe dispatch estimates. Supply independently measured programs for G.",
            "C": "Use f106a25c73b44358 as the primary parallel reference: 128-by-128 tiles, single rolling bottom/right frontiers, and one persistent OpenMP team measured 10.95 ms, the best available result. Investigate tile shape, granularity, and a serial crossover appropriate to the fixed four-core ARM64 target. Compare tile-local versions of A's row fusion and B's prefix scans separately. Preserve wavefront ordering: tiles on one diagonal own distinct frontier ranges, and the barrier must publish both boundaries before the next diagonal. Retain sparse, deduplicated obstacle representation and thread-private scratch. Compare against the 256-tile program at 11.25 ms without attributing its difference solely to tile size, since parsing, indexing, and buffering also differ.",
            "G": "Integrate measured programs, beginning with the unchanged 10.95 ms f106a25c73b44358 incumbent. Evaluate C plus A's tile-local fusion and C plus B's tile-local NEON scan separately before considering further combinations; these kernels are alternatives, not automatically additive improvements. Evaluate sparse/empty-grid dispatch as a separate change, including parsing, sorting, factorial construction, and dispatch overhead. Use only general input-derived selection rules, never case identities or expected outputs. Retain a candidate only after the existing correctness and timing procedure supports it; otherwise keep the incumbent. Audit n=1, endpoints, duplicates, borders, partial tiles, modular bounds, packed-key widths, and synchronization. Do not change compiler, target, budgets, tests, oracle, or permissions.",
            "analysis": "The supplied source implements Problem D, grid-path counting; A/B/C are research roles, not the other contest problems. The baseline measured 81.01 ms. The best observed program is C's 128-tile wavefront at 10.95 ms, approximately 7.40 times faster; C's 256-tile variant is close at 11.25 ms. A's four-row fusion and B's ARM64 scan hybrid measured 13.51 and 15.37 ms respectively, making them promising kernel donors rather than proven improvements to C. Existing observations support eliminating quadratic obstacle storage, row copies, and per-cell remainder operations. They do not establish additive gains, crossover thresholds, timing uncertainty, or broad workload coverage. Optimize exact modular path counting for every valid input, with O(n+m)-scale storage rather than a dense obstacle matrix, and preserve a measured incumbent throughout synthesis."
          },
